{
	title: 'Startup cost follows open tabs, not sessions on disk',
	description: 'Every startup that becomes host runs recovery (continue unfinished turns, run queued messages an old host left behind). Its cost grows with the open tabs, never with the number of sessions on disk: it reads no metadata or history of closed sessions (closing a tab does not stop its turn today; if a closed session can hold an unfinished turn or queued messages, the host keeps a small durable list of such sessions rather than scanning), and for open ones it reads marks.ason (task bq) rather than history tails wherever the marks cover the file. Queued messages are found from the durable record of undelivered inbox messages (marks.ason keeps their offsets), so a queued message is never missed however far back it sits; the old 64 KiB-tail guess goes. Budget: with 2,000 closed sessions and 100 open idle tabs on disk, recovery finishes in under 10 ms on an M1-class machine; a test measures it (counting files and bytes read, not only time). Tests: a queued message written 1 MB before the end of history runs after a host restart; a turn still running in a closed tab is continued after a restart.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['bq'],
	foldInto: ['2r', 'bq', 'j1']
}
